 "text": "Skipping just today, twice a week, costs you 104 days a year. Why do we stop doing things we know are good for us? Reason 20. Every skip feels like one day, Tuesday you're tired, Thursday you're busy. But you never make that choice once, you make it again and again. Two a week is 104 days a year, three months of your goal gone. Researchers call this choice bracketing, judge the pattern not the day. Before you skip ask, would I be okay skipping this every week? If not, do the 10-minute version, it still counts as a day. Same tired Tuesday, this time the day is kept. Send this to the friend who skips just today every week. That's what demure is for, archive your dream today and break it into small steps you can actually do. Follow for one reason a day, next, why the practice that feels best teaches you least. Which day do you usually skip, the links in the bio.",
